WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:09.810
Examples of encryption persist throughout human

2
00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:12.369
history from early cryptic depictions by

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.840
cave dwellers of Magura cave in Bulgaria to the pyramids at Giza.

4
00:00:17.229 --> 00:00:21.200
Even then each group had its own primitive cryptographic approach

5
00:00:21.209 --> 00:00:24.159
so that members of the tribe or group could communicate

6
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.409
with one another while keeping secrets from the rival tribes

7
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.459
regarding hunting grounds or sources of water and food.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.759
It is part of human nature to encrypt information.

9
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:38.340
You start with clear text, which is the information that you and I could easily read

10
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.360
and then you use an algorithm which is often a

11
00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:43.970
form of software that can be embedded in the system,

12
00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:47.459
but that needs to be activated with an encryption key.

13
00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:52.500
A very simple example is if you are trying to encrypt a PDF document, for example,

14
00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:54.459
perhaps your accountant is sending you some

15
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.970
documents to sign before submitting your taxes.

16
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.009
Encryption would create a cipher text which no one can use.

17
00:01:01.069 --> 00:01:04.919
And you and your accountant would have set up a preset encryption key

18
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.730
so that you could retrieve the information at either end of the communication,

19
00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:13.760
you need to have good key management, which means you safeguard the information

20
00:01:13.959 --> 00:01:17.680
because imagine if you have thousands of keys in a commercial environment,

21
00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.739
there is often a third party or external server

22
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.389
where the keys will be separately stored and managed.

23
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.480
So you don't have all your eggs in one basket, so to speak,

24
00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.330
it will be protected through a hashing system

25
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.160
and no one else can have access to those keys.

26
00:01:32.849 --> 00:01:36.050
Asymmetric encryption is more secure because the sender

27
00:01:36.059 --> 00:01:38.519
and receiver each uses a unique code,

28
00:01:38.529 --> 00:01:39.879
often a certificate.

29
00:01:40.029 --> 00:01:42.720
So you can confirm that the information has been sent

30
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.639
from the sender to the recipient in a secure manner.